\section{All-center clearance and a true physical reference}
\label{sec:sc-clearance}
For a record $Q$ put $S_i(Q)=[Q_i,Q_{i+1}]$. Define the true third-disk clearance of a flight by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sc-clearance-definition}
 \Gamma_i(Q)=\inf_{z\in\Lambda\setminus\{O_i,O_{i+1}\}}
          \operatorname{dist}(z,S_i(Q))-R,\qquad
 \Gamma(Q)=\min_{0\le i<m}\Gamma_i(Q).
\end{equation}
The infimum includes disks visited elsewhere in the word. It excludes only the two incident centers of this particular flight. Distances are Euclidean distances to closed segments, so a minimizing foot may change or reach an endpoint. There is no chosen differentiable nearest-center label.

\begin{sctheorem}[One modulus for every third lattice disk]\label{thm:sc-clearance}
For every $Y\in\mathcal S(Q)$ and $d(E)=22E^{1/4}$,
\begin{align}
 d_H(S_i(Y),S_i(Q))&\le d(E),\label{eq:sc-hausdorff}\\
 \big|\operatorname{dist}(z,S_i(Y))-\operatorname{dist}(z,S_i(Q))\big|
      &\le d(E)\quad\text{for every }z\in\mathbb R^2,\label{eq:sc-all-centers}\\
 |\Gamma_i(Y)-\Gamma_i(Q)|&\le d(E).\label{eq:sc-min-clearance}
\end{align}
All these estimates hold simultaneously over all flights and all lattice centers. They require no lower bound on any interior incidence.
\end{sctheorem}
\begin{proof}
Match points with the same affine segment parameter $s\in[0,1]$. Their difference is $(1-s)\delta_i+s\delta_{i+1}$, whose norm is at most $\max\{|\delta_i|,|\delta_{i+1}|\}\le d(E)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:sc-quarter}. This bounds both directed Hausdorff distances. For two nonempty compact sets at Hausdorff distance at most $d$, the triangle inequality gives $\operatorname{dist}(z,A)\le\operatorname{dist}(z,B)+d$ and the reverse inequality, proving \eqref{eq:sc-all-centers}. Take infima over the \emph{same} center set in both directions to obtain \eqref{eq:sc-min-clearance}. The infima are finite. No counting or differentiability of the minimizing center is used.
\end{proof}

\begin{sctheorem}[A grazing-independent clearance certificate for a physical minimum]\label{thm:sc-physical}
Let $Q$ be any original regular record, and put
\[
 \varepsilon(E)=\sqrt{2E/\tau_-},\qquad d(E)=22E^{1/4}.
\]
Suppose
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sc-certificate}
 c_i(Q)>\varepsilon(E)\ (1\le i<m),\qquad \Gamma(Q)>d(E).
\end{equation}
The first condition is vacuous for $m=1$. Then $A$ has exactly one global minimum on $\mathcal K$. This minimum is an actual regular exterior first-hit trajectory of the same ordered collision word, with normal initial and final endpoints. All these assertions concern the unchanged radius $R$ and actual circular obstacles. They assert no equality of its section/return labels or physical roof with those of $Q$.
\end{sctheorem}
\begin{proof}
Compactness supplies at least one minimizing $Y$. Since $A(Y)\le A(Q)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:sc-quarter} and Lemma~\ref{lem:sc-gap} apply. Each edge error obeys
\[
 |\widehat v_i-v_i|\le\sqrt{2E/\ell_i}\le\varepsilon(E).
\]
All edges are nonzero because consecutive disks are separated. The gradient of $A$ with respect to an interior vertex is
$\widehat d_i=\widehat v_{i-1}-\widehat v_i$. By the physical reflection law of $Q$ and \eqref{eq:sc-certificate},
\[
 |\widehat d_i|\ge2c_i-|e_{i-1}|-|e_i|\ge2c_i-2\varepsilon(E)>0.
\]
First-order optimality on the $i$th closed disk means $Y_i$ minimizes its linear functional $\widehat d_i\cdot z$. Hence
$Y_i=O_i+R\widehat n_i$ with
$\widehat d_i=-|\widehat d_i|\widehat n_i$. For two unit vectors $a,b$ with $a-b=-\lambda n$, $\lambda>0$, taking the scalar product with $a+b$ shows $a\cdot n=-b\cdot n$; taking it with $n$ gives $a\cdot n=-\lambda/2$ and $b\cdot n=\lambda/2$. Their tangential components are equal. This proves a true nontangent specular reflection at each interior vertex, with the correct exterior signs. At the endpoints the gradients are $-\widehat v_0$ and $\widehat v_{m-1}$, of norm one. Their disk minimizers give $\widehat n_0=\widehat v_0$ and $\widehat n_m=-\widehat v_{m-1}$, precisely normal endpoints.

Theorem~\ref{thm:sc-clearance} and the strict second inequality in \eqref{eq:sc-certificate} give $\Gamma_i(Y)>0$ for every flight. Thus no third disk meets any flight, including a disk appearing at a different time in the word. The two incident disks are avoided in the open flight as well: positive departing normal component implies
$|Y_i+s\widehat v_i-O_i|^2=R^2+2Rs(\widehat n_i\cdot\widehat v_i)+s^2>R^2$ for $s>0$, and the analogous backward formula applies at arrival. This proves first-hit physicality without discarding any collision.

For uniqueness, use this physical $Y$ as the reference in \eqref{eq:sc-positive-gap}. Its normal endpoints have defect zero. Any other minimum $Z$ has $A(Z)=A(Y)$, so the nonnegative gap is zero. All contact coefficients are strictly positive (one at each endpoint and $2\widehat c_i$ internally), and \eqref{eq:sc-disk-curvature} forces $Z_i=Y_i$ at every vertex. Thus the minimum is unique.
\end{proof}

\begin{scremark}[Sufficiency, not source deletion or capacity]
The test may fail while a physical minimum still exists. Failed records remain in the original source. The incidence requirement in \eqref{eq:sc-certificate} checks nonzero comparison reflections; it is not an inverse-incidence loss in the clearance modulus. An actual critical reference is one trajectory, not a positive measure comparison pool. Exact section visits and return labels must be evaluated anew on it, not transported from $Q$ by proximity. The original observed roof remains $L(Q)$, never $A(Y)$.
\end{scremark}

\begin{scproposition}[Positive inclusion on the unchanged exact-record source]\label{prop:sc-source}
Fix $R,m$ and any of the original exact return/displacement/collision events $\Pi$, with its original section probability measure $\nu_R^*$ and nonnegative measurable weight $h$. Let $\mathcal B$ be the subset of regular records whose ordered disk word does not have a unique regular physical normal-endpoint global minimum. Set
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sc-witness}
 \mathcal W=\left\{Q:\ \exists i\in\{1,\ldots,m-1\},\ c_i(Q)\le\varepsilon(E(Q))
       \quad\text{or}\quad\Gamma(Q)\le d(E(Q))\right\}.
\end{equation}
On the regular source, $\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal W$. Consequently the following positive measure inequality holds on the \emph{original physical roof axis}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sc-source-order}
 L_*\bigl(\mathbf1_{\mathcal B}\mathbf1_\Pi h\,d\nu_R^*\bigr)
 \ \le\
 L_*\bigl(\mathbf1_{\mathcal W}\mathbf1_\Pi h\,d\nu_R^*\bigr).
\end{equation}
This includes all physical words with one-copy multiplicity and extends to nonnegative marked sums by monotone convergence. It changes neither the conditioned event nor any roof observation window. It asserts no small upper bound on the right-hand side.
\end{scproposition}
\begin{proof}
The inclusion is the contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:sc-physical}. At fixed $R,m$ the physical ordered-word collection is countable; the property defining $\mathcal B$ is a property of a word, not of a chosen minimizer. Thus $\mathcal B$ is a union of its measurable regular word cylinders. The functions in \eqref{eq:sc-witness} are measurable on every regular chart: each center-to-segment distance is continuous, the center set is countable, and finite minima and the displayed elementary functions preserve measurability. No measurable selection of a minimum is needed.

Multiply the pointwise inclusion by $\mathbf1_\Pi h\mathbf1_{\{L\in I\}}$ for any Borel roof set $I$, then integrate. This gives \eqref{eq:sc-source-order}, initially for bounded $h$ and then by increasing truncation. Apply the same argument on disjoint original marked copies and use monotone convergence for complete nonnegative sums. The section normalization is the original one already in $\nu_R^*$; no additional Jacobian or copy factor appears.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Exact remaining analytic obligation}
If the two pushforwards are absolutely continuous, \eqref{eq:sc-source-order} orders their densities almost everywhere at the same $R,m,n,k,t$. Without that hypothesis it remains a measure inequality, not an assertion that a singular component vanishes. Paired boundary traces are not bulk measures and are not set to zero by this argument.

The positive witness source in \eqref{eq:sc-source-order} is \emph{not} proved to be small in $m^2L^\infty(dt)$. Nor are all physical-reference records shown to meet the original nongrazing comparison-pool constraints. The original first-order single/cross-flight clearance, low-load incidence source, degenerate/critical charts, section cuts and singular pairing terms therefore remain in \eqref{eq:sc-original-obligation}. The present theorem supplies a proved geometric mechanism for obtaining a physical reference without the previous inverse-incidence clearance loss. A normalized source/pressure or original-capacity argument must still exploit that mechanism, with the original full sums and order of limits, to close the raw theorem.
